// URI references, RFC 3986:
// https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3986
//
// Taking one apart, putting it back, resolving a relative one against a base,
// and the percent coding that lets any octet travel inside one. A form body, a
// request target, a `Location` and a data URL all obey this grammar.
//
// Percent coding takes the component as a parameter. Which octets may stand for
// themselves is RFC 3986's answer; three callers writing that list by hand is
// three chances to get it wrong.
//
// Not here: internationalised host names (RFC 3492), and what belongs to the
// WHATWG URL Standard rather than to RFC 3986 — special schemes, default ports,
// Windows drive letters.
import {
  "moonbitlang/core/encoding/utf8",
}

import {
  "moonbitstack/moonhttp/url",
} for "test"
